= Distributed artificial intelligence — Simple =

'''Distributed artificial intelligence''' (DAI) studies AI systems in which several computers or agents share intelligent work.

Instead of one central system doing everything, different agents can:

* observe different parts of the environment;
* solve different parts of a problem;
* communicate;
* negotiate;
* divide tasks;
* combine results.

This is useful when information or control is naturally spread across several places.

== Distributed problem solving ==

One early form of DAI is '''distributed problem solving'''.

A large problem is divided into smaller parts:

<pre>
large problem
→ split into subtasks
→ several agents work
→ combine results
</pre>

This can make work faster when the subtasks can be solved mostly independently.

If every subtask depends on every other one, communication can become more expensive than the parallelism is worth.

== Multi-agent systems ==

A [[Multi-agent system]] contains several agents that can make at least some decisions independently.

Agents may:

* cooperate;
* compete;
* have partly different goals.

A fleet of warehouse robots might share a delivery goal while each robot decides how to avoid nearby obstacles.

An automated marketplace can contain agents with conflicting goals.

== Task allocation ==

A distributed system needs to decide who does what.

One historical method is the '''Contract Net Protocol'''.<ref name="contract">[https://doi.org/10.1109/TC.1980.1675516 Smith, R. G. (1980), “The Contract Net Protocol.”]</ref>

A manager agent announces a task.

Other agents can bid.

The manager selects a contractor.

This allows task assignment without one computer permanently controlling every resource.

== Local knowledge ==

Different agents may know different things.

One robot may see an obstacle that others cannot see.

One software agent may control a private database.

This is related to [[Dispersed knowledge]].

The system has to coordinate without assuming every agent knows the full state.

== Communication costs something ==

Agents can exchange messages, but messages require:

* bandwidth;
* time;
* energy;
* processing.

Sending every observation to every agent can become inefficient.

DAI therefore asks what information really needs to be shared.

== Messages can be late ==

Distributed agents do not always update at exactly the same time.

A message can arrive late or be lost.

An agent may act using old information.

Protocols have to decide how much synchronization is necessary.

== Consensus ==

Sometimes agents need to agree on a value.

They may repeatedly exchange estimates until they converge.

Agreement is useful for coordination.

But:

<pre>
agreement
does not automatically mean
the agreed answer is true
</pre>

All agents can share the same mistake.

== Distributed constraint optimization ==

In a distributed constraint optimization problem, different agents control different variables.

They cooperate to find assignments that satisfy constraints and optimize an overall objective.

This is used in scheduling, smart grids, sensor networks, and resource allocation.<ref name="dcop">[https://jair.org/index.php/jair/article/view/11146 Fioretto, Pontelli and Yeoh (2018), “Distributed Constraint Optimization Problems and Applications.”]</ref>

== Partial observability ==

Agents may see only part of the state.

That makes planning difficult.

Each agent has to act using its own history while accounting for what other agents might know.

Optimal decentralized planning can be computationally much harder than centralized planning.<ref name="decpomdp">[https://doi.org/10.1287/moor.27.4.819.297 Bernstein et al. (2002), “The Complexity of Decentralized Control of Markov Decision Processes.”]</ref>

== Multi-agent learning ==

Agents can also learn.

One agent may change its behavior while another is learning at the same time.

This makes the environment look unstable.

Agents may have to learn:

* communication;
* cooperation;
* competition;
* roles;
* conventions.

A strategy that works only with the same training partners may fail with new agents.

== Fault tolerance ==

Distribution can make a system more robust.

If one agent fails, another may take over.

But this only works if the system actually has redundancy.

A “distributed” system with one essential central coordinator still has a single point of failure.

== Security ==

Agents can send incorrect or malicious messages.

A secure system needs:

* authenticated identities;
* authorization;
* message integrity;
* validation.

Being another agent in the system is not enough reason to trust a message.

== DAI is not just distributed computing ==

A neural network trained across several GPUs is distributed computing.

It is not necessarily distributed artificial intelligence in the classic DAI sense.

DAI usually focuses on distributed reasoning, planning, learning, autonomy, or coordination among agents.

== Artificial agents can share the same error ==

Several AI agents may appear independent while using the same underlying model.

If that model is wrong, all agents can repeat the same error.

Ten agreeing agents are not ten independent confirmations.

